1) Why playwright is better than selenium ?
Ans- Playwright is better than Selenium because it solves many of the pain points we faced with Selenium. It’s easier to set up since it doesn’t require separate browser drivers—browser binaries are installed automatically. It has built-in auto-waiting and web-first assertions, which greatly reduce flaky tests. Playwright also supports parallel execution and cross-browser testing using a single API, and it comes with default HTML reports and powerful debugging tools like trace viewer and video recordings. Overall, it improves test stability, execution speed, and developer productivity compared to Selenium.

2) What are the practices should be followed for building the playwright framework ?
Ans- While building a Playwright automation framework, I follow standard design and test automation best practices to keep the framework maintainable, scalable, and stable. I follow principles like DRY to avoid duplication, and I design reusable components using Page Object Model. I rely on Playwright’s built-in fixtures instead of implementing Singleton patterns, since Playwright already handles test isolation and browser lifecycle efficiently. I also follow SOLID principles where applicable, keep configuration and environment handling centralized, use meaningful test naming and tagging, and ensure proper reporting, logging, and CI integration.

3) How to achieve Test Isolation ?
Ans- Test isolation in Playwright is achieved through browser contexts. Playwright Test creates a new browser context for each test by default. Each context has its own cookies, local storage, session storage, permissions, and cache, so no state is shared between tests. Because of this, tests can run independently and in parallel without affecting each other. 

4) How will you run 100 testcae efficiently in playwright ?
To run 100 test cases efficiently in Playwright, I primarily rely on parallel execution using workers, which can be configured in the Playwright config file. Playwright runs tests in isolated browser contexts, allowing safe parallel execution. I also use tagging to group tests such as smoke, regression, or critical, so only relevant tests are executed when needed. Additionally, I run tests in headless mode for faster execution, use retries for flaky tests, and leverage Playwright’s default HTML reporting to analyze failures efficiently. In CI environments, sharding can be used to distribute tests across multiple machines for even faster execution.

5) Difference between page, context, and browser? When would you use each?
In Playwright, the browser represents a real browser instance like Chromium, Firefox, or WebKit and is the top-level object that you launch once per run. A browser context is an isolated environment inside that browser, similar to an incognito window, with its own cookies, local storage, cache, and permissions, which makes it ideal for test isolation. A page is a single tab within a context where actual interactions like clicking, typing, and navigation happen. Typically, you create one browser per test run, one context per test to keep data isolated, and one or more pages inside that context depending on whether your test needs multiple tabs.

6) How does Playwright auto-wait work? What problems does it solve?
Playwright automatically waits before performing actions like click, fill, or expect by ensuring the element is attached to the DOM, visible, enabled, and stable. It also waits for navigations and network idle when appropriate. This auto-waiting removes the need for most explicit waits and sleep statements, making tests more reliable. It solves common problems such as timing issues, race conditions, and flaky failures caused by elements not being ready when the action is attempted.

7) How do you handle flaky tests in Playwright?
Flaky tests are handled by first identifying the root cause, which is usually timing issues, unstable locators, animations, or test data dependencies. In Playwright, using robust locators like role or text-based locators, relying on auto-wait instead of manual timeouts, and adding proper assertions with expect helps a lot. You can also use retries in the test config, enable tracing for failed runs, and make sure tests are isolated with fresh contexts. Fixing the cause is preferred over just adding retries, which should be a safety net.

8) What is strict mode in locators? How do you debug strict mode violations?
Strict mode means that a locator used for actions must resolve to exactly one element, otherwise Playwright throws an error. This prevents tests from accidentally interacting with the wrong element when multiple matches exist. When a strict mode violation happens, you debug it by inspecting the error message that lists matched elements, using page.pause() or the Playwright inspector, and refining the locator with better selectors like getByRole, adding filters, or using nth() only when truly needed.

9) Difference between locator() and page.$()?
page.locator() does not immediately fetch the element. It creates a locator that re-queries the DOM and supports auto-waiting and retries when used with actions or assertions. whereas page.$() immediately returns an ElementHandle from the DOM and does not auto-wait for actions, so it can easily become stale if the DOM changes. Locators are the recommended modern API for writing stable Playwright tests

10) How do you handle file upload and download?
For file uploads, Playwright provides setInputFiles on file input elements, which allows you to upload one or more files without interacting with the OS file picker. For downloads, you listen for the download event using page.waitForEvent('download') inside Promise.all() to avoid race conditions, then save the file using download.saveAs()  and validate it via filesystem APIs.
 
11) How do you test multiple tabs or windows?
In Playwright, multiple pages are handled using browser contexts. Each test runs inside its own browser context. A context can have one or more pages (tabs or windows). Each context is completely isolated, with its own cookies, local storage, permissions, and cache. Because of this isolation, tests can safely run in parallel without affecting each other.

12) How do you handle a new tab / new window?
When an action opens a new tab or window, I listen for the page event on the browser context using context.waitForEvent('page') and capture the new page object which and switch control using bringToFront() for any visual UI interactions.

13) How do you create a new browser context and page?
I create a new browser context using browser.newContext() to isolate session state such as cookies, cache, and storage. Then I open a fresh page using context.newPage(). This ensures test isolation and prevents cross-test contamination.”
